IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `fpa-dates`
IMPORT `fpa-rules`

-- Family Provision Act 1969 (ACT) -- the questions of the family member who claims provision out of a deceased
-- person's estate.
--
-- SEAT: the individual who applies, or is thinking of applying, to the ACT Supreme Court for provision out of the estate
-- of a deceased person: the partner, the person in a domestic relationship, the child, the stepchild, the grandchild or
-- the parent. "May I apply?", "By when must I apply?", "Can the court order provision for me?", "Whom must I serve?",
-- "Can my provision be increased?", "Can it come out of property already handed out?", "Can I sue the administrator?",
-- "How does the order take effect?". The Supreme Court, the administrator and the public trustee get no seat: what they
-- may do is asked as a fact ("if the court is satisfied that ...").
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. The one duty with a deadline the claimant meets (apply within 6 months after the grant, s 9) is a regulative
-- rule in fpa-rules.l4, run by EVALTRACE inside the time question.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 4 (grants sealed in the ACT, as the date of the grant), s 7 (who may apply, with the s 7 (9)
-- definition of partner and the s 7 (7) test of being maintained), s 8 (1), (2) (the order and the court's
-- satisfaction), s 9 (the time limit and extensions), s 9A (3), (4) (increasing my provision; notice of an application to
-- cut it), s 10 (1) (service), s 13 (1) (property under a power of appointment), s 14 (presumption of death), s 16
-- (operation of the order), s 19 (dealings with the provision), s 20 (property already distributed), s 21 (the
-- administrator's protection), s 22 (2) (the deceased's written reasons). Out, and why: NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Family Provision Act 1969, A1969-15, Republication No 12 (R12), effective 12 December 2023, deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/A1969-15.txt. The Act sets its own numbers (6 months, 2 years); no regulation is needed.
--
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to the Supreme Court are INPUTS ("if the
-- court is satisfied ..."). Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): JUST FALSE
-- is the text's own no; NOTHING is where the text is limited by its own wording, or silent.

§ `Family Provision Act 1969: the questions of the family member claiming provision`

-- The facts s 7 turns on, gathered into one record inside the module. The exported questions take them one by one.
DECLARE `Family facts` HAS
    `was a domestic partner`               IS A BOOLEAN
    `was a spouse or civil partner`        IS A BOOLEAN
    `years as domestic partner`            IS A NUMBER
    `is a parent of the deceased's child`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `years in a domestic relationship`     IS A NUMBER
    `is a child`                           IS A BOOLEAN
    `is a stepchild`                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `is a grandchild`                      IS A BOOLEAN
    `is a parent`                          IS A BOOLEAN
    `maintenance order in force`           IS A BOOLEAN
    `was being maintained`                 IS A BOOLEAN
    `a court could order maintenance`      IS A BOOLEAN
    `my linking parent died first`         IS A BOOLEAN
    `a parent of mine survived`            IS A BOOLEAN
    `maintained by a parent`               IS A BOOLEAN
    `survived by a partner`                IS A BOOLEAN
    `survived by a child`                  IS A BOOLEAN

-- The paragraphs of s 7 (1).
DECLARE `Head of s 7 (1)` IS ONE OF
    `s 7 (1) (a): a partner of the deceased`
    `s 7 (1) (b): a person in a domestic relationship with the deceased for 2 or more years continuously`
    `s 7 (1) (c): a child of the deceased`
    `s 7 (1) (d): a stepchild of the deceased, maintained by the deceased immediately before death`
    `s 7 (1) (e): a grandchild of the deceased who meets s 7 (3)`
    `s 7 (1) (f): a parent of the deceased who meets s 7 (4)`

§§ `s 7 -- who may apply`

-- s 7 (9): "partner, of a deceased person, means someone who -- (a) was the domestic partner of the person at any time;
-- and (b) either -- (i) was the person's spouse, civil union partner or civil partner at any time; or (ii) was the
-- person's domestic partner continuously for 2 or more years at any time; or (iii) is the parent of a child of the
-- person." "Domestic partner" is borrowed from the Legislation Act 2001 s 169 (not deposited): asked as a fact.
GIVEN f IS A `Family facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a partner under s 7 (9)` f IF
        f's `was a domestic partner`
    AND (   f's `was a spouse or civil partner`
         OR f's `years as domestic partner` AT LEAST 2
         OR f's `is a parent of the deceased's child`)

-- s 7 (7): "a person shall not be regarded as having been maintained by the deceased person immediately before his or
-- her death unless -- (a) there was in force at that time an order of a court requiring the deceased person to pay
-- maintenance ...; or (b) the deceased person was, at that time, whether under a written agreement or otherwise,
-- maintaining that other person or making a contribution to the maintenance of that other person, being a contribution
-- that ... can be regarded as other than a nominal contribution; or (c) a court would, if the deceased person were still
-- living, have power to make an order requiring the deceased person to pay maintenance ...".
-- READING (fork F3): any of (a)-(c) is taken as enough; the text states them as necessary conditions only ("shall not
-- be regarded ... unless"), and the other reading still asks whether the person was in fact maintained.
GIVEN f IS A `Family facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `was maintained by the deceased under s 7 (7)` f IF
        f's `maintenance order in force`
    OR  f's `was being maintained`
    OR  f's `a court could order maintenance`

-- s 7 (3): a grandchild may apply only if "(a) the parent of the grandchild who was a child of the deceased person died
-- before the deceased person died; or (b) a parent of the grandchild was alive on the day the deceased person died but the
-- grandchild, immediately before the deceased person died -- (i) was not maintained by a parent; and (ii) was maintained
-- by the deceased person."
GIVEN f IS A `Family facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `meets s 7 (3)` f IF
        f's `my linking parent died first`
    OR  (    f's `a parent of mine survived`
         AND (NOT (f's `maintained by a parent`))
         AND `was maintained by the deceased under s 7 (7)` f)

-- s 7 (4): a parent may apply only if "(a) the parent was maintained by the deceased person immediately before his or her
-- death; or (b) the deceased person was not survived by any partner or any of the children of the deceased person."
-- READING: (b) holds when the deceased left neither a partner nor any child.
GIVEN f IS A `Family facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `meets s 7 (4)` f IF
        `was maintained by the deceased under s 7 (7)` f
    OR  (    (NOT (f's `survived by a partner`))
         AND (NOT (f's `survived by a child`)))

-- Puts a paragraph at the front of the list if its test holds.
GIVEN `the test` IS A BOOLEAN, `the head` IS A `Head of s 7 (1)`, `the rest` IS A LIST OF `Head of s 7 (1)`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Head of s 7 (1)`
`add if` `the test` `the head` `the rest` MEANS
    IF `the test` THEN `the head` FOLLOWED BY `the rest` ELSE `the rest`

-- The paragraphs of s 7 (1) the person qualifies under, in the order of the section.
GIVEN f IS A `Family facts`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Head of s 7 (1)`
`the s 7 heads for` f MEANS
    `add if` `test a` `head a` (`add if` `test b` `head b` (`add if` `test c` `head c` (`add if` `test d` `head d` (`add if` `test e` `head e` (`add if` `test f` `head f` EMPTY)))))
    WHERE
        `test a` MEANS `is a partner under s 7 (9)` f
        `test b` MEANS (NOT (`is a partner under s 7 (9)` f)) AND f's `years in a domestic relationship` AT LEAST 2
        `test c` MEANS f's `is a child`
        `test d` MEANS f's `is a stepchild` AND `was maintained by the deceased under s 7 (7)` f
        `test e` MEANS f's `is a grandchild` AND `meets s 7 (3)` f
        `test f` MEANS f's `is a parent` AND `meets s 7 (4)` f
        `head a` MEANS `s 7 (1) (a): a partner of the deceased`
        `head b` MEANS `s 7 (1) (b): a person in a domestic relationship with the deceased for 2 or more years continuously`
        `head c` MEANS `s 7 (1) (c): a child of the deceased`
        `head d` MEANS `s 7 (1) (d): a stepchild of the deceased, maintained by the deceased immediately before death`
        `head e` MEANS `s 7 (1) (e): a grandchild of the deceased who meets s 7 (3)`
        `head f` MEANS `s 7 (1) (f): a parent of the deceased who meets s 7 (4)`

-- s 7 (1)-(4): may the person apply?
--   JUST TRUE  -- the person falls under a paragraph of s 7 (1) and meets any "unless" in s 7 (2)-(4);
--   JUST FALSE -- the person is a stepchild, grandchild or parent who fails s 7 (2), (3) or (4) and falls under no other
--                 paragraph (the text's no: "is not entitled ... unless");
--   NOTHING    -- the person is none of the listed relatives, or falls short of a limit in the list's own wording (a
--                 domestic relationship under 2 years, a domestic partner who is not a "partner"): the s 7 (1) list is
--                 not closed with "only" (ruling (c) of 2026-11-05).
GIVEN f IS A `Family facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the s 7 answer for` f MEANS
    CONSIDER `the s 7 heads for` f
    WHEN EMPTY THEN
        IF f's `is a stepchild` OR f's `is a grandchild` OR f's `is a parent`
        THEN JUST FALSE
        ELSE NOTHING
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

GIVEN `domestic partner` IS A BOOLEAN, `spouse or civil partner` IS A BOOLEAN, `years as domestic partner` IS A NUMBER,
      `parent of their child` IS A BOOLEAN, `years in a domestic relationship` IS A NUMBER, `child` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `stepchild` IS A BOOLEAN, `grandchild` IS A BOOLEAN, `parent` IS A BOOLEAN, `maintenance order` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `maintained` IS A BOOLEAN, `court could order maintenance` IS A BOOLEAN, `linking parent died first` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `a parent survived` IS A BOOLEAN, `maintained by a parent` IS A BOOLEAN, `left a partner` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `left a child` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Family facts`
`the family facts` `domestic partner` `spouse or civil partner` `years as domestic partner` `parent of their child` `years in a domestic relationship` `child` `stepchild` `grandchild` `parent` `maintenance order` `maintained` `court could order maintenance` `linking parent died first` `a parent survived` `maintained by a parent` `left a partner` `left a child` MEANS
    `Family facts` WITH
        `was a domestic partner`               IS `domestic partner`
        `was a spouse or civil partner`        IS `spouse or civil partner`
        `years as domestic partner`            IS `years as domestic partner`
        `is a parent of the deceased's child`  IS `parent of their child`
        `years in a domestic relationship`     IS `years in a domestic relationship`
        `is a child`                           IS `child`
        `is a stepchild`                       IS `stepchild`
        `is a grandchild`                      IS `grandchild`
        `is a parent`                          IS `parent`
        `maintenance order in force`           IS `maintenance order`
        `was being maintained`                 IS `maintained`
        `a court could order maintenance`      IS `court could order maintenance`
        `my linking parent died first`         IS `linking parent died first`
        `a parent of mine survived`            IS `a parent survived`
        `maintained by a parent`               IS `maintained by a parent`
        `survived by a partner`                IS `left a partner`
        `survived by a child`                  IS `left a child`

@export May I apply to the ACT Supreme Court for provision out of a deceased person's estate (Family Provision Act 1969 s 7)? A partner, a person in a domestic relationship with the deceased for 2 or more years, a child, a stepchild the deceased maintained, a grandchild who meets s 7 (3), and a parent who meets s 7 (4) may apply. Yes, no (the Act's own "not entitled ... unless"), or nothing (you are not one of the people the Act lists).
GIVEN `domestic partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you the deceased's domestic partner (living with them as a couple, or their spouse, civil union partner or civil partner) at any time?
      `spouse or civil partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you the deceased's spouse, civil union partner or civil partner at any time?
      `years as domestic partner` IS A NUMBER @desc The longest time, in years, you were continuously the deceased's domestic partner (0 if never)
      `parent of their child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you the parent of a child of the deceased (a child you had together)?
      `years in a domestic relationship` IS A NUMBER @desc The longest time, in years, you were continuously in a domestic relationship with the deceased (a personal relationship, as a couple or otherwise, in which one of you gave the other personal or financial commitment and support; Domestic Relationships Act 1994 s 3); 0 if never
      `child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a child of the deceased (including a child born alive after the death, s 7 (8))?
      `stepchild` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a stepchild of the deceased?
      `grandchild` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a grandchild of the deceased?
      `parent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a parent of the deceased?
      `maintenance order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Immediately before the death, was a court order in force requiring the deceased to pay maintenance to you or for your benefit?
      `maintained` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Immediately before the death, was the deceased maintaining you, or contributing more than a nominal amount to your maintenance (under a written agreement or otherwise)?
      `court could order maintenance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would a court, if the deceased were still alive, have power to order the deceased to pay maintenance to you or for your benefit?
      `linking parent died first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are a grandchild: did your parent who was the deceased's child die before the deceased? (No if you are not a grandchild)
      `a parent survived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are a grandchild: was a parent of yours alive on the day the deceased died?
      `maintained by a parent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are a grandchild: immediately before the deceased died, were you maintained by a parent of yours?
      `left a partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the deceased leave a surviving partner (a spouse, civil union partner or civil partner, a domestic partner of 2 or more years, or a domestic partner who is the parent of the deceased's child)?
      `left a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did any child of the deceased survive the deceased?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 7 -- may I apply for provision out of the estate` `domestic partner` `spouse or civil partner` `years as domestic partner` `parent of their child` `years in a domestic relationship` `child` `stepchild` `grandchild` `parent` `maintenance order` `maintained` `court could order maintenance` `linking parent died first` `a parent survived` `maintained by a parent` `left a partner` `left a child` MEANS
    `the s 7 answer for` (`the family facts` `domestic partner` `spouse or civil partner` `years as domestic partner` `parent of their child` `years in a domestic relationship` `child` `stepchild` `grandchild` `parent` `maintenance order` `maintained` `court could order maintenance` `linking parent died first` `a parent survived` `maintained by a parent` `left a partner` `left a child`)

@export Under which paragraphs of s 7 (1) of the Family Provision Act 1969 (ACT) may I apply for provision out of a deceased person's estate? An empty list means none.
GIVEN `domestic partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you the deceased's domestic partner (living with them as a couple, or their spouse, civil union partner or civil partner) at any time?
      `spouse or civil partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you the deceased's spouse, civil union partner or civil partner at any time?
      `years as domestic partner` IS A NUMBER @desc The longest time, in years, you were continuously the deceased's domestic partner (0 if never)
      `parent of their child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you the parent of a child of the deceased (a child you had together)?
      `years in a domestic relationship` IS A NUMBER @desc The longest time, in years, you were continuously in a domestic relationship with the deceased (Domestic Relationships Act 1994 s 3); 0 if never
      `child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a child of the deceased (including a child born alive after the death, s 7 (8))?
      `stepchild` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a stepchild of the deceased?
      `grandchild` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a grandchild of the deceased?
      `parent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a parent of the deceased?
      `maintenance order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Immediately before the death, was a court order in force requiring the deceased to pay maintenance to you or for your benefit?
      `maintained` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Immediately before the death, was the deceased maintaining you, or contributing more than a nominal amount to your maintenance?
      `court could order maintenance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would a court, if the deceased were still alive, have power to order the deceased to pay maintenance to you or for your benefit?
      `linking parent died first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are a grandchild: did your parent who was the deceased's child die before the deceased?
      `a parent survived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are a grandchild: was a parent of yours alive on the day the deceased died?
      `maintained by a parent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are a grandchild: immediately before the deceased died, were you maintained by a parent of yours?
      `left a partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the deceased leave a surviving partner?
      `left a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did any child of the deceased survive the deceased?
GIVETH A LIST OF `Head of s 7 (1)`
`s 7 -- the paragraphs I may apply under` `domestic partner` `spouse or civil partner` `years as domestic partner` `parent of their child` `years in a domestic relationship` `child` `stepchild` `grandchild` `parent` `maintenance order` `maintained` `court could order maintenance` `linking parent died first` `a parent survived` `maintained by a parent` `left a partner` `left a child` MEANS
    `the s 7 heads for` (`the family facts` `domestic partner` `spouse or civil partner` `years as domestic partner` `parent of their child` `years in a domestic relationship` `child` `stepchild` `grandchild` `parent` `maintenance order` `maintained` `court could order maintenance` `linking parent died first` `a parent survived` `maintained by a parent` `left a partner` `left a child`)

§§ `s 8 -- the family provision order`

-- s 8 (1): "On application by a person entitled, under section 7, to apply ..., the Supreme Court may order that the
-- provision as that court thinks fit be made for the applicant out of the estate."
-- s 8 (2): the court "shall only make an order ... if satisfied, in consideration of the criteria set out in subsection
-- (3), that as at the date of the order, adequate provision for the proper maintenance, education or advancement in life
-- of the applicant is not available -- (a) under the will ...; or (b) if the deceased died intestate -- under the law
-- applicable to that intestacy; or (c) under that will and that law combined."
-- The court's satisfaction is a judgment: asked as a fact. The s 8 (3) criteria are listed in the @desc.
--   JUST TRUE  -- s 7 is met and the court is satisfied: the court may order provision (how much is for the court);
--   JUST FALSE -- s 7's own "unless" fails, or the court is not satisfied (s 8 (2) "shall only ... if");
--   NOTHING    -- the person is not one of the people s 7 (1) lists.
@export Can the ACT Supreme Court order provision for me out of the deceased's estate (Family Provision Act 1969 s 8)? Only if you may apply under s 7 and the court is satisfied that, at the date of the order, adequate provision for your proper maintenance, education or advancement in life is not available under the will, the intestacy rules, or both. The court weighs the s 8 (3) criteria: your character and conduct; the nature and length of your relationship with the deceased; contributions to property and to welfare (as homemaker or parent too); income, property and resources; capacity to work; financial needs and obligations; responsibilities to support others; Domestic Relationships Act property orders; maintenance payments between you; and anything else relevant.
GIVEN `domestic partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you the deceased's domestic partner (living with them as a couple, or their spouse, civil union partner or civil partner) at any time?
      `spouse or civil partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you the deceased's spouse, civil union partner or civil partner at any time?
      `years as domestic partner` IS A NUMBER @desc The longest time, in years, you were continuously the deceased's domestic partner (0 if never)
      `parent of their child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you the parent of a child of the deceased (a child you had together)?
      `years in a domestic relationship` IS A NUMBER @desc The longest time, in years, you were continuously in a domestic relationship with the deceased (Domestic Relationships Act 1994 s 3); 0 if never
      `child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a child of the deceased (including a child born alive after the death, s 7 (8))?
      `stepchild` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a stepchild of the deceased?
      `grandchild` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a grandchild of the deceased?
      `parent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a parent of the deceased?
      `maintenance order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Immediately before the death, was a court order in force requiring the deceased to pay maintenance to you or for your benefit?
      `maintained` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Immediately before the death, was the deceased maintaining you, or contributing more than a nominal amount to your maintenance?
      `court could order maintenance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would a court, if the deceased were still alive, have power to order the deceased to pay maintenance to you or for your benefit?
      `linking parent died first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are a grandchild: did your parent who was the deceased's child die before the deceased?
      `a parent survived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are a grandchild: was a parent of yours alive on the day the deceased died?
      `maintained by a parent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are a grandchild: immediately before the deceased died, were you maintained by a parent of yours?
      `left a partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the deceased leave a surviving partner?
      `left a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did any child of the deceased survive the deceased?
      `the court is satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Supreme Court satisfied, weighing the s 8 (3) criteria, that adequate provision for your proper maintenance, education or advancement in life is not available to you under the will, the intestacy rules, or both? (The court's judgment: answer as the case may turn out)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 8 -- can the court order provision for me` `domestic partner` `spouse or civil partner` `years as domestic partner` `parent of their child` `years in a domestic relationship` `child` `stepchild` `grandchild` `parent` `maintenance order` `maintained` `court could order maintenance` `linking parent died first` `a parent survived` `maintained by a parent` `left a partner` `left a child` `the court is satisfied` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the s 7 answer for` (`the family facts` `domestic partner` `spouse or civil partner` `years as domestic partner` `parent of their child` `years in a domestic relationship` `child` `stepchild` `grandchild` `parent` `maintenance order` `maintained` `court could order maintenance` `linking parent died first` `a parent survived` `maintained by a parent` `left a partner` `left a child`)
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST `the court is satisfied`
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

§§ `s 9 -- the time for applying (with s 4)`

-- s 9 (1) with s 4 (2): the last day to apply.
@export What is the last day to apply for provision out of a deceased person's estate (Family Provision Act 1969 s 9 (1))? The application must be made within 6 months after the date administration (probate or letters of administration) is granted in the ACT; for a grant made elsewhere and sealed by the ACT Supreme Court, after the date of sealing (s 4). The Supreme Court can extend the time (s 9 (2)).
GIVEN `the grant` IS A DATE @desc The date probate or letters of administration of the estate were granted in the ACT (or, for a grant made outside the ACT, the date it was sealed by the ACT Supreme Court), e.g. 2026-03-10
GIVETH A DATE
`s 9 -- the last day to apply` `the grant` MEANS `the last day of the 6 months after` `the grant`

DECLARE `Time to apply` IS ONE OF
    `the 6 months have not started: no administration of the estate has been granted in the ACT yet`
    `applied in time`
    `not yet applied, still in time`
    `out of time: you need the Supreme Court to extend the time (s 9 (2)), which it may do whether or not the time has ended`
    `out of time, and no extension can now be asked for: the estate has been lawfully and fully distributed (s 9 (4))`

-- s 9 (1)-(5): am I in time?
-- The last day is the end of the 6 months, or a later day to which the court has extended the time (s 9 (2), (3)).
-- s 9 (5): the application is made on the day the document instituting it is filed.
-- READING (fork F4): a filing before the grant is counted as made on the day of the grant (day 0), not as out of time.
-- s 9 (4): "An application for the extension ... shall not be made after the estate ... has been lawfully and fully
-- distributed": a flat negative, the text's no.
@export Am I in time to apply for provision out of the deceased's estate (Family Provision Act 1969 s 9)? You must apply within 6 months after the grant of administration, or within the time the Supreme Court has extended it to. If you are out of time you can ask the court to extend it, but not after the estate has been lawfully and fully distributed.
GIVEN `granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has probate or letters of administration of the estate been granted in the ACT (or a grant from elsewhere sealed by the ACT Supreme Court)?
      `the grant` IS A DATE @desc The date of the grant (or of the sealing), e.g. 2026-03-10; any date if there is none yet
      `applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you filed your application (the notice of motion or other document starting it)?
      `applied on` IS A DATE @desc The date you filed it; any date if you have not
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `extended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Supreme Court extended the time for your application?
      `extended to` IS A DATE @desc If so, the last day of the extended time; any date if not
      `fully distributed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the estate been lawfully and fully distributed?
GIVETH A `Time to apply`
`s 9 -- am I in time to apply` `granted` `the grant` `applied` `applied on` `today` `extended` `extended to` `fully distributed` MEANS
    IF (NOT `granted`)
    THEN `the 6 months have not started: no administration of the estate has been granted in the ACT yet`
    ELSE IF `applied`
         THEN IF `filed in time under s 9` `the days allowed` `the day filed`
              THEN `applied in time`
              ELSE `the out-of-time answer`
         ELSE IF `the last day` `falls before` `today`
              THEN `the out-of-time answer`
              ELSE `not yet applied, still in time`
    WHERE
        `the six months end` MEANS `the last day of the 6 months after` `the grant`
        `the last day` MEANS
            IF `extended` AND `the six months end` `falls before` `extended to`
            THEN `extended to`
            ELSE `the six months end`
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the days from` `the grant` `to` `the last day`
        `the raw day` MEANS `the days from` `the grant` `to` `applied on`
        `the day filed` MEANS IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw day`
        `the out-of-time answer` MEANS
            IF `fully distributed`
            THEN `out of time, and no extension can now be asked for: the estate has been lawfully and fully distributed (s 9 (4))`
            ELSE `out of time: you need the Supreme Court to extend the time (s 9 (2)), which it may do whether or not the time has ended`

-- s 9 (2)-(4): may I still ask for an extension?
--   JUST FALSE -- the estate has been lawfully and fully distributed (s 9 (4), a flat negative);
--   JUST TRUE  -- otherwise: the court "may ... extend the time", "whether or not the time ... has ended" (s 9 (3) (b)).
@export Can I still ask the ACT Supreme Court to extend the time for my family provision application (Family Provision Act 1969 s 9 (2)-(4))? Yes, even after the 6 months have ended, unless the estate has been lawfully and fully distributed. Whether it extends, and on what conditions, is for the court.
GIVEN `fully distributed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the estate been lawfully and fully distributed?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 9 -- can I still ask for more time` `fully distributed` MEANS
    IF `fully distributed` THEN JUST FALSE ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `ss 9A, 10 -- after applying, and changing an order`

-- s 10 (1): "If an application has been made ... for an order under section 8 or 9A ..., the applicant shall cause notice
-- of the application to be served on each person who is an administrator of the estate". No time is set. s 10 (2): the
-- court may order service on others (its decision: named in the @desc).
-- Fork F5 (a duty whose "if" is unmet, decided case by case): no application made -> the duty does not arise (FALSE).
@export Must I have notice of my family provision application served on the administrator of the estate (Family Provision Act 1969 s 10)? Yes, once you have applied under s 8 (or s 9A): on each administrator (executor or person granted letters of administration). The court may also order you to serve anyone else it thinks fit.
GIVEN `applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you applied to the Supreme Court for a family provision order (under s 8), or to increase one (under s 9A)?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 10 -- must I serve the administrators` `applied` IF `applied`

-- s 9A (3): "if by a previous order the Supreme Court has directed that provision by way of periodical payments or the
-- benefit of the investment of a lump sum be made for a person ..., on application made by or on behalf of the person, if
-- the court is satisfied that the provision is not adequate ..., the court may ... vary the previous order by increasing
-- the amount of the provision." "Previous order" means an order under this Act not discharged (s 9A (1)).
--   JUST TRUE  -- such an order exists and the court is satisfied: it may increase the provision;
--   JUST FALSE -- no such order, or the court is not satisfied (the text's "if").
@export Can the ACT Supreme Court increase the provision ordered for me (Family Provision Act 1969 s 9A (3))? Only if an earlier order (not discharged) gave you provision by periodical payments or the benefit of an invested lump sum, you (or someone for you) apply, and the court is satisfied the provision is not adequate for your proper maintenance, education or advancement in life.
GIVEN `periodic or invested provision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does an order made for you under the Act (and not discharged) give you provision by periodical payments or by the benefit of the investment of a lump sum?
      `the court is satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Supreme Court satisfied that this provision is not adequate for your proper maintenance, education or advancement in life? (The court's judgment)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 9A (3) -- can my provision be increased` `periodic or invested provision` `the court is satisfied` MEANS
    JUST (`periodic or invested provision` AND `the court is satisfied`)

-- s 9A (4): "The applicant for an order under subsection (2) shall cause notice of the application to be served on the
-- person in whose favour the previous order was made." From the claimant's seat: must I be told?
@export Must I be served with notice if someone applies to reduce, suspend or discharge the family provision order made for me (Family Provision Act 1969 s 9A (2), (4))? Yes: the administrator or a beneficiary who applies must have notice served on you.
GIVEN `an order in my favour` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an order for provision been made for you under the Act, and not discharged?
      `someone applied to cut it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the administrator or a beneficiary applied to reduce, suspend or discharge that order?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 9A (4) -- must I be served with an application to cut my provision` `an order in my favour` `someone applied to cut it` IF
    `an order in my favour` AND `someone applied to cut it`

§§ `ss 13, 20, 21 -- where the provision can come from`

-- s 13 (1): if "(a) application is made under section 8 or 9A ...; and (b) the deceased person has, by will, exercised a
-- general or a special power of appointment in respect of property, being a power under which the deceased person was,
-- immediately before death, entitled to appoint the property to himself or herself; and (c) the Supreme Court is
-- satisfied that -- (i) adequate provision for the person who has made the application cannot justly be made out of other
-- property forming part of the estate ...; or (ii) because of the existence of special circumstances, an order should be
-- made ...; the court may order that provision be made out of, or charged on, the property". s 13 (2), (3) count property
-- passing under a general devise or bequest as "other property" for (c) (i): part of the court's judgment, in the @desc.
@export Can my provision come out of property the deceased appointed by will under a power of appointment (Family Provision Act 1969 s 13)? Yes, if you have applied, the deceased exercised by will a general or special power under which they could have appointed the property to themselves immediately before death, and the court is satisfied either that adequate provision cannot justly be made out of the rest of the estate (which counts real or personal property passing under a general devise or bequest, s 13 (2), (3)) or that special circumstances call for it.
GIVEN `applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you applied for a family provision order (under s 8 or s 9A)?
      `a power exercised by will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the deceased, by will, exercise a general or special power of appointment over the property, being a power under which they could have appointed the property to themselves immediately before death?
      `the rest is not enough` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Supreme Court satisfied that adequate provision for you cannot justly be made out of the other property in the estate? (The court's judgment)
      `special circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Supreme Court satisfied that, because of special circumstances, provision should be made out of (or charged on) the appointed property? (The court's judgment)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 13 -- can my provision come from appointed property` `applied` `a power exercised by will` `the rest is not enough` `special circumstances` MEANS
    JUST (`applied` AND `a power exercised by will` AND (`the rest is not enough` OR `special circumstances`))

-- s 20 (1): "notwithstanding any distribution of property forming part of the estate ... made by the administrator ..., the
-- Supreme Court may, in an order under section 8 or 9A ..., direct that provision be made for a person out of that
-- property", except where s 20 (2) bars it (fpa-rules.l4).
--   JUST FALSE -- s 20 (2) bars it ("shall not direct");
--   JUST TRUE  -- otherwise the court may direct it.
@export Can my provision come out of estate property the administrator has already distributed (Family Provision Act 1969 s 20)? Yes, unless the distribution was properly made for the maintenance, education or advancement of someone who depended on the deceased, or it was made more than 6 months after the grant, before the administrator had notice of your application (or of your application for more time), and the property has vested in possession of someone.
GIVEN `made for a dependant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the distribution properly made to provide for the proper maintenance, education or advancement in life of someone who was wholly or partly dependent on the deceased immediately before the death?
      `the grant` IS A DATE @desc The date administration of the estate was granted (or sealed in the ACT), e.g. 2026-03-10
      `distributed on` IS A DATE @desc The date the property was distributed
      `notice first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Before the distribution, had the administrator had notice of your application (or of your application to extend the time to apply)?
      `vested` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the distributed property vested in possession of anyone (been handed over so that it is theirs to have now)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 20 -- can distributed property be used for my provision` `made for a dependant` `the grant` `distributed on` `notice first` `vested` MEANS
    IF `s 20 (2) bars the distributed property` `made for a dependant` `the grant` `distributed on` `notice first` `vested`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- s 21: "An action does not lie against the administrator ... because of his or her having distributed the whole or any
-- part of the estate ... if the distribution was a distribution referred to in section 20 (2) or if -- (a) the
-- distribution was made before the administrator had notice of an application for an order under this Act or notice of
-- an application to extend the time ...; and (b) before making the distribution, the administrator had given notice in
-- accordance with the Administration and Probate Act 1929, section 64 and the time specified in the notice for sending
-- in claims had expired."
--   JUST FALSE -- the administrator is protected (a flat negative: "An action does not lie");
--   NOTHING    -- otherwise: the Act does not say an action lies; that is for the general law.
@export Can I sue the administrator for distributing the estate before my family provision claim was dealt with (Family Provision Act 1969 s 21)? No, if the distribution is one s 20 (2) protects, or if it was made before the administrator had notice of your application (or of your application for more time) and after the administrator had given the Administration and Probate Act 1929 s 64 notice and the time in it for sending in claims had expired. Otherwise the Act does not say; the general law decides.
GIVEN `made for a dependant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the distribution properly made to provide for the proper maintenance, education or advancement in life of someone who was wholly or partly dependent on the deceased immediately before the death?
      `the grant` IS A DATE @desc The date administration of the estate was granted (or sealed in the ACT), e.g. 2026-03-10
      `distributed on` IS A DATE @desc The date the property was distributed
      `notice first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Before the distribution, had the administrator had notice of your application (or of your application to extend the time to apply)?
      `vested` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the distributed property vested in possession of anyone?
      `s 64 notice given and expired` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Before the distribution, had the administrator given notice under the Administration and Probate Act 1929 s 64 (the advertisement for claims), and had the time it gave for sending in claims expired?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 21 -- can I sue the administrator for distributing` `made for a dependant` `the grant` `distributed on` `notice first` `vested` `s 64 notice given and expired` MEANS
    IF     `s 20 (2) bars the distributed property` `made for a dependant` `the grant` `distributed on` `notice first` `vested`
        OR ((NOT `notice first`) AND `s 64 notice given and expired`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `ss 14, 16, 19, 22 -- the order and what follows`

-- s 14: where the grant was made on a presumption of death, the court "may direct that the provision shall not be made
-- unless the person in whose favour the order is made gives an undertaking or security" to restore property (or, at the
-- person's option, pay its value at the time received) and repay money received, if the grant is revoked because the
-- deceased was alive. The direction is the court's: asked as a fact.
@export Must I give an undertaking or security before my provision is paid, because the grant was made on a presumption of death (Family Provision Act 1969 s 14)? Only if the grant of administration was made on the presumption that the person is dead and the court directs it.
GIVEN `presumed dead` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was administration of the estate granted on evidence that the person may be presumed dead (rather than on proof of death)?
      `the court directs it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Supreme Court directed that your provision is not to be made unless you give an undertaking or security?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 14 -- must I give an undertaking before I am paid` `presumed dead` `the court directs it` IF
    `presumed dead` AND `the court directs it`

-- s 14 (a), (b): what the undertaking covers, if the grant is revoked because the person was alive: money received is
-- repaid in full; property other than money is restored, or "at his or her option" its value at the time received is paid.
@export How much would I have to pay back under a s 14 undertaking if the person presumed dead turns out to have been alive (Family Provision Act 1969 s 14)? The money you received under the order, plus, if you choose to pay rather than return property you received, its value when you received it.
GIVEN `money received` IS A NUMBER @desc The money (in dollars) you received under the order
      `property value` IS A NUMBER @desc The value (in dollars), at the time you received it, of property other than money you received under the order (0 if none)
      `I would pay instead` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would you choose to pay the value of that property rather than return the property itself?
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 14 -- what I would have to pay back` `money received` `property value` `I would pay instead` MEANS
    IF `I would pay instead`
    THEN `money received` PLUS `property value`
    ELSE `money received`

DECLARE `How the order operates` IS ONE OF
    `as if it were a codicil to the deceased's will, made immediately before death (s 16 (1))`
    `as a modification of the Administration and Probate Act 1929 part 3A (the intestacy rules) for that property (s 16 (2))`

-- s 16: "(1) Subject to subsection (2), an order under section 8 operates as if it were a codicil to the will of the
-- deceased person executed by the deceased person immediately before death. (2) An order under section 8 in relation to
-- property of a deceased person who died intestate operates as a modification of the Administration and Probate Act 1929,
-- part 3A in its application to that property."
-- READING (fork F6): asked property by property; property that passes on intestacy (wholly or partly) takes s 16 (2).
@export How does a family provision order made for me take effect on the deceased's property (Family Provision Act 1969 s 16)? As if it were a codicil to the will, made just before death; or, for property that passes on intestacy, as a change to the intestacy rules for that property.
GIVEN `passes on intestacy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the property the order is about pass on intestacy (no will, or a will that does not deal with it)?
GIVETH A `How the order operates`
`s 16 -- how my order takes effect` `passes on intestacy` MEANS
    IF `passes on intestacy`
    THEN `as a modification of the Administration and Probate Act 1929 part 3A (the intestacy rules) for that property (s 16 (2))`
    ELSE `as if it were a codicil to the deceased's will, made immediately before death (s 16 (1))`

-- s 19: "A mortgage, charge or assignment of any kind whatsoever, of or over the provision made, or to be made, by an
-- order under this Act, is of no force or effect unless that mortgage, charge or assignment is made with the permission
-- of the Supreme Court."
--   JUST FALSE -- without the court's permission (the text's "unless");
--   NOTHING    -- with permission (fork F8: an "unless" once met, decided case by case): s 19 no longer stands in the way,
--                 but it does not itself make the dealing effective.
@export Is a mortgage, charge or assignment I give over my family provision effective (Family Provision Act 1969 s 19)? Not unless it is made with the Supreme Court's permission. With permission, s 19 no longer stands in the way.
GIVEN `with permission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the mortgage, charge or assignment made with the Supreme Court's permission?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 19 -- is my dealing with the provision effective` `with permission` MEANS
    IF `with permission` THEN NOTHING ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 22 (1): the court "shall ... have regard to the testator's reasons, so far as they are ascertainable". s 22 (2): it
-- "may receive in evidence a statement signed by the testator and purporting to bear the date when it was signed and to
-- set out reasons for making or not making provision ... for a person as evidence of those reasons." s 22 (3): its weight
-- is for the court.
--   JUST TRUE  -- a signed statement that purports to be dated and to give reasons: the court may receive it;
--   NOTHING    -- otherwise: s 22 (2) does not reach it, but the court still has regard to the reasons so far as they can
--                 be found out another way (s 22 (1)).
@export Can a written statement by the deceased explaining why they left me out (or left me little) be used as evidence (Family Provision Act 1969 s 22)? The court may receive a statement signed by the deceased that purports to bear the date it was signed and to set out the reasons; how much weight it gets is for the court. The court must in any case have regard to the deceased's reasons so far as they can be found out.
GIVEN `signed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the statement signed by the deceased?
      `dated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does it purport to bear the date it was signed?
      `gives reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does it purport to set out the deceased's reasons for making, or not making, provision (or further provision) for you in the will?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 22 -- can the deceased's statement of reasons be evidence` `signed` `dated` `gives reasons` MEANS
    IF `signed` AND `dated` AND `gives reasons` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING
